FastSASA Notices
================

FastSASA is a GPU-accelerated SASA implementation with Vulkan, CUDA, and
threaded CPU backends. It builds on FreeSASA (Mitternacht S. FreeSASA: An open
source C library for solvent accessible surface area calculations.
F1000Research 2016, 5:189): FastSASA reproduces FreeSASA's formulation of
the Shrake-Rupley and Lee-Richards calculations so that results agree with it,
and it reuses FreeSASA data, output formats, and test fixtures as listed below.
FastSASA binaries do not link libfreesasa.

FreeSASA
--------

Copyright (c) 2016 Simon Mitternacht. Distributed under the MIT License; the
full text is kept in `licenses/FreeSASA-radii-table-MIT.txt` and must be
included with source and binary redistributions that contain this material.

Material from FreeSASA 2.1.3 in this repository:

- Radius table. The standard residue/atom mappings in `share/protor.config`
  (also compiled into the library), and the same base mappings reused by
  `share/protor_glycans.config`, are the generated ProtOr configuration
  distributed by FreeSASA. The underlying ProtOr radii are from Tsai J,
  Taylor R, Chothia C, Gerstein M. J Mol Biol. 1999;290:253-266. The glycan
  mappings in `share/protor_glycans.config` are a FastSASA opt-in extension;
  that file is not an unmodified upstream file.
- Relative SASA references. The per-residue maximum-ASA values used for
  relative SASA (`--format rsa`, in `tools/fastsasa_cli.cc`) are FreeSASA's
  ProtOr reference values.
- Output formats. The RSA, PDB (B-factor/REMARK), log, residue, and sequence
  outputs and their messages in `tools/fastsasa_cli.cc` reproduce FreeSASA's
  formats for drop-in compatibility.
- Lee-Richards arc routine. The CPU, CUDA, and Vulkan Lee-Richards kernels
  follow FreeSASA's `sasa_lr.c`: the same contact tests in the same order and
  the same arc wrapping (including its `inf`/`sup` naming).
- Tables and reference values. The backbone atom list in
  `tools/fastsasa_cli.cc`, the mmCIF column table in `src/gemmi_topology.cc`,
  and the 1UBQ reference values in `tests/cli_validation.py` come from
  FreeSASA.
- Test fixtures. The structure files in `tests/data/` (other than the 1UBQ
  XTC/DCD trajectories noted below) are FreeSASA 2.1.3's test fixtures,
  unmodified.

The benchmark tooling in `tools/manuscript_benchmark_2026_freesasa_driver/`
downloads FreeSASA 2.1.3, applies a small patch (a NULL-pointer check for
isolated atoms), and links it, for comparisons only; it is not part of the
FastSASA library or binaries.

FreeBSD msun / musl (acos, atan, atan2)
---------------------------------------

`src/fastsasa_exact_math.h` (CPU reference and CUDA FP64 kernels) and
`shaders/vulkan/lee_richards_cell.comp` (Vulkan FP64 Lee-Richards shader,
embedded as SPIR-V in every Vulkan-enabled binary) contain double-precision
acos, atan and atan2 routines derived from the FreeBSD msun sources
(e_acos.c, s_atan.c, e_atan2.c) as distributed by musl. All backends run the
same operation sequence, so their FP64 results are bit-identical.

